<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.3.4: Dividing Polynomial Functions and the Remainder Theorem</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01608</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.3.4: Dividing Polynomial Functions and the Remainder Theorem</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3ffc3387-05f0-4b88-a8f9-9526092a7241</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity introduces the division of polynomial functions. Polynomial functions can be divided just as polynomials have been in the previous activities. Division involving polynomial functions can be represented in the format \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\) or \(\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\).</para>
<para>This lesson represents an extension from the TEK listed and can be used as an enrichment to enhance the students’ understanding of dividing polynomials.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Tell students that now that they have practiced dividing polynomials, polynomial functions can be divided in the same way.</para>
<para>Allow 7 – 10 minutes for students to complete questions 1 – 3 in the activity. If there is any confusion, solve the first question as a class.</para>
<para>Introduce the Remainder Theorem:</para>
<list>
  <item>If a polynomial function \(f(x)\) is divided by \(x-c\), then the remainder is \(f(c)\).</item>
</list>
<para>Stress to students that the divisor must be in the form \(x-c\), as was the case for synthetic division. By simply finding the value, \(f(c)\), the remainder can be determined. Allow students 5 – 7 minutes to solve questions 4 – 5.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Divide the polynomial functions. Find the quotient value.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(f(x)=x^2-5x-24\) 
    \(g(x)=x+3\)
    
    \((\frac{f}{g})(x) =\) ___________
    
    \(f(-3) =\) ___________
  </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> </para>
<para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-8\)
\(f(-3)=0\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(f(x)=x^2-15x+54\)
    \(g(x)=x-9\)
    
    \((\frac{f}{g})(x) =\) ___________
    
    \(f(9) =\) ___________
  </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
<para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-6\)
\(f(9)=0\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(f(x)=x^2+2x-3\)
    \(g(x)=x+3\)
    
    \((\frac{f}{g})(x) =\) ___________
    
    \(f(-3) =\) ___________
  </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> </para>
<para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-1\)
\(f(-3)=0\)</para>
<para>The Remainder Theorem states that if a polynomial function \(f(x)\) is divided by \(x-c\), then the remainder is \(f(c)\). This means we can always check the remainder by finding \(f(c)\) when the divisor is written in the form \(x-c\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=x^3-7x+12\) is divided by \(x + 3\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>6 </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=2x^3-6x-24\) is divided by \(x - 3\).</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 12 </para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Students should be familiar with polynomial division by this activity. Emphasize that the process of dividing polynomial functions is the same as they learned in previous activities. Students should remember that for functions \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\), where \(g(x) \neq 0\), \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\).</para>
<para>Ask the class to explain the Remainder Theorem. Emphasize the following key points:</para>
<list>
  <item>The Remainder Theorem states if a polynomial function \(f(x)\) is divided by \(x - c\), then the remainder is \(f(c)\). </item>
  <item> When finding the value of \(c\), the divisor must be written in the form \(x - c\). This means that for a divisor of \(x + 6\), the value of \(c\) would be \(-6\). </item>
</list>
<title>6.3.4: Self Check</title>
<!--BEGIN SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->   

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>

<!--SELF CHECK QUESTION GOES BEFORE THE Table -->   

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>For functions \(f(x)=x^3+2x^2-19x+12\) and \(g(x)=x-3\):</para>
<para>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\) and \((\frac{f}{g})(0)\).</para>

<!--SELF CHECK table-->
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
   <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
 </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x^2+5x-4\)</para>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(0)=-4\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: Multiply \((x^2+5x-4)(x-3)\) to get \(x^3+2x^2-19x+12\). The answer is correct.</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x^2+5x-4\)</para>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(0)=12\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the value you found for \((\frac{f}{g})(0)\). You found \(f(0)\). The answer is \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x^2+5x-4\) and \((\frac{f}{g})(0)=-4\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x^2+5x+4\)</para>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(0)=4\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the signs in your division. The answer is \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x^2+5x-4\) and \((\frac{f}{g})(0)=-4\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x^2-5x+4\)</para>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(0)=-4\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the signs in your division. The answer is \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x^2+5x-4\) and \((\frac{f}{g})(0)=-4\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>

<!--END SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->   

<title>6.3.4: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
<title>Dividing Polynomial Functions</title>
<para>Just as polynomials can be divided, polynomial functions can also be divided.</para>

<div>
  <para><emphasis> DIVISION OF POLYNOMIAL FUNCTIONS </emphasis></para>
  <hr/>
  <para>For functions \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\), where \(g(x) \neq 0\),</para>
  <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\)</para>
</div>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>For functions \(f(x)=x^2-5x-14\) and \(g(x)=x+2\):</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\)  Substitute for \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\).</para>
<para>  \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)=x^2-5x-14}{x+2}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Divide the polynomials using long division.</para>
<para>  \[\begin{array}{l}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,x - 7\\x + 2\left){\vphantom{1{{x^2} - 5x - 14}}}\right.
  \!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{{x^2} - 5x - 14}}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ({x^2} + 2x)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, - 7x - 14\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ( - 7x - 14)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\]
  \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-7\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> In part (1), we found \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\).</para>
<para>  \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x−7\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - 
  </emphasis>To find \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)\), substitute \(x=-4\).</para>
<para>  \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)=-4-7\)
  \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)=-11\)</para>

<title>Try It: Dividing Polynomial Functions</title>
<para>For functions \(f(x)=x^2-5x-36\) and \(g(x)=x+4\):</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\). </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(-5)\). </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>Solution </emphasis>button to compare your work.</para>
<title> Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to solve these polynomial function division problems:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((\frac{f}{g})(x) = \frac{x^2-5x-36}{x+4}=x-9\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((\frac{f}{g})(-5)=x-9=(-5)-9=-14\) </item>
</list>
<title>Using the Remainder Theorem</title>
<para>Let’s look at some division problems and their remainders. They are summarized in the chart below. If we take the dividend from each division problem and use it to define a function, we get the functions shown in the chart. When the divisor is written as \(x - c\), the value of the function at \(c\), \(f(c)\), is the same as the remainder from the division problem.</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavyadjustedtable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Dividend</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Divisor</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Remainder</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Function</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(f(c)\)</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x^4-7x^2+7x+6\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x - (-3)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(3\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(x)=x^4-7x^2+7x+6\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(-3)=3\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(x)=3x^3-2x^2-10x+8\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x - (-3)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(-61\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(x)=3x^3-2x^2-10x+8\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(-3)=-61\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x^4-16x^2+3x+15\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x - (-4)\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(3\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(x)=x^4-16x^2+3x+15\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>\(f(-4)=3\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>To see this more generally, we realize we can check a division problem by multiplying the quotient times the divisor and adding the remainder. In function notation, we could say: To get the dividend \(f(x)\), we multiply the quotient, \(q(x)\), times the divisor, \(x-c\), and add the remainder, \(r\).</para>
<para>\(f(x)=q(x)(x-c)+r\)</para>
<para> If we evaluate this at \(c\), we get:</para>
<para> \(f({\style{color:red}c})=q({\style{color:red}c})({\style{color:red}c}-c)+r\)
  \(f(c)=q(c)(0)+r\) 
  \(f(c)=r\)</para>
<para>This leads us to the Remainder Theorem.</para>

<note>
  <para><emphasis>REMAINDER THEOREM</emphasis></para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If the polynomial function \(f(x)\) is divided by \(x-c\), then the remainder is \(f(c)\).</para>
</note>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>
<para>Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\) is divided by \(x + 2\).</para>
<para>To use the Remainder Theorem, we must use the divisor in the \(x-c\) form. We can write the divisor \(x+2\) as \(x-(-2)\). So, our \(c\) is \(-2\).</para>
<para>To find the remainder, we evaluate \(f(c)\), which is \(f(-2)\).</para>
<para>\(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> To evaluate \(f(-2)\), substitute \(x=-2\).</para>
<para>  \(f({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})=({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})^3+3({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})+19\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Simplify. </para>
<para>  \(f(-2)=(-8)-6+19\) 
  \(f(-2)=5\)
  The remainder is 5 when \(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\) is divided by \(x+2\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check using synthetic division.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/05a284d4b51db6421214ef8ac2c1d86317bb15a1"/></media></figure></para>
<title>Try It: Using the Remainder Theorem</title>
<para>Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=x^3+4x+15\) is divided by \(x + 2\).</para>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to solve a problem using the Remainder Theorem:</para>
<para>\(f(x)=x^3+4x+15\)
  \(f(2)=(-2)^3+4(-2)+15\) 
  \(f(2)=-8-8+15= -1\) </para>
<para>The remainder is \(-1\).</para></section>
